"{\n \"meta\": {\n  \"board\": \"Aula MINI 60 HE PRO via 2.4GHz dongle\",\n  \"vid\": \"0x0C45\",\n  \"pid\": \"0xFEFE\",\n  \"collection\": {\n   \"usagePage\": \"0xFF60\",\n   \"usage\": \"0x61\",\n   \"reportId\": 0,\n   \"outBytes\": 32,\n   \"inBytes\": 32\n  },\n  \"frameLen\": 32,\n  \"payloadChunk\": 24,\n  \"capturedAt\": \"2026-07-21\",\n  \"what\": \"The vendor app's OWN connect handshake over RF, captured with the tap armed before open() and mirrored to localStorage so the mid-flow page reload could not lose it. 47 OUT frames each paired with an IN reply.\",\n  \"sequence\": \"0x10 x3 (paged device info, 0x18/0x18/0x08 = 56B) -> 0x12 x22 (key table, 528B) -> 0x13 x1 (lighting state) -> 0x14 x21 (per-key RGB table, 504B)\",\n  \"notes\": [\n   \"The 0x14 reply returns records [id,R,G,B] = FF 00 00 for ids 0,1,2 — the solid red written by an earlier Aether test. Persistent per-key storage survives a link close/open, read back over RF.\",\n   \"A raw open() of the device does NOT produce this traffic: the handshake lives in the app's own connect path, so it must be triggered through the UI (checkDevice -> device card).\",\n   \"No battery-specific command appears in the handshake even though the UI shows a battery percentage — it likely rides the 0x10 device-info reply or an unsolicited input report.\"\n  ],\n  \"devices\": [\n   {\n    \"vid\": 3141,\n    \"pid\": 65278,\n    \"name\": \"MINI 60 HE PRO Dongle\",\n    \"collections\": [\n     {\n      \"usagePage\": 65376,\n      \"usage\": 97,\n      \"outBytes\": [\n       32\n      ],\n      \"inBytes\": [\n       32\n      ]\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"vid\": 3141,\n    \"pid\": 65278,\n    \"name\": \"MINI 60 HE PRO Dongle\",\n    \"collections\": [\n     {\n      \"usagePage\": 65376,\n      \"usage\": 97,\n      \"outBytes\": [\n       32\n      ],\n      \"inBytes\": [\n       32\n      ]\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"vid\": 3141,\n    \"pid\": 65278,\n    \"name\": \"MINI 60 HE PRO Dong